+# Source Orchestrator/Template Acceptance
+
+Contract: `milestone-loop-orchestrator-source.v1`. Authority epoch: `orch-template.v1`. Claim scope: `orchestrator-template`. The machine-readable twin is `evals/acceptance-manifest.json`.
+
+## Evidence rules and gates
+
+Every required observation is tied to the exact source commit/tree, authority/lock/manifest hashes, run ID, platform, runtime, command argv, and execution-provider identity. Workflow evidence separately identifies the disposable fixture/adopter commit and authority; those identities must never be substituted for the source identity. Every successful child command owns a validated receipt with nonempty required artifacts, sizes, and SHA-256 hashes. Verification independently checks those artifacts and the actual before/after Git, state, process, and filesystem observations. Exit zero, a unit-test report, a self-asserted status, or a fabricated sandbox identity is insufficient.
+
+The public command handlers and shared production controller perform all transitions. Workflow drivers may set up fresh repositories and supply deterministic, separately executed role responses. They must not seed a completed state, mutate private refs as the supposed operation, generate fake child receipts, accept a review on the worker's behalf, or override isolation/eligibility guards. Each scenario reports whether its role transport is deterministic or live. Unit/regression tests remain mandatory supporting evidence but cannot replace the workflow observations below.
+
+`ORCH-AUTONOMOUS-READINESS-01` requires all twelve machine requirements, all metrics, all sixteen negative scenarios, both supported platforms, the exact no-argument source verification gate, and no identity/authority drift. Source artifact eligibility is scoped to this source contract only. Missing, unavailable, skipped, flaky, timed-out, or mismatched evidence remains non-passing. An expected rejection is a successful negative scenario only when the intended boundary actually executes, rejects for the specified reason, and leaves the specified state intact; an earlier missing-runtime failure cannot satisfy it.
+
+`ORCH-HUMAN-ACCEPT-01` follows machine readiness and requires the live operator workflow below. All gates use logical AND without averaging or compensation. No migration or proposal-integrity report is a product acceptance receipt.
+
+## Required machine outcomes
+
+| ID | Real workflow and passing outcome | Required evidence and negative control |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| ORCH-STATIC-01 | In an isolated clean checkout, install exact frozen workspace dependencies and verify the actual graph, package-manager/runtime pins, formatting, lint, strict typechecking, and every discovered regression test. Analyze actual imports and executable entrypoints against the controller/worker/evidence/adapter boundary allowlist. | Lock/store/installed-graph report, import-edge report, raw test inventories, receipts. A corrupted dependency/undeclared import and an unknown test must fail; a nonempty output directory or successful install alone does not prove graph correctness. |
+| ORCH-BUILD-01 | `pnpm build` invokes the declared `build:production` and emits the portable release payload described below. Two independent clean builds per platform yield byte-identical payload inventories. From a detached extraction with the original checkout inaccessible, install its frozen dependencies and run its shipped CLI to generate and commission a new adopter. | Build argv and clean-clone report; sorted payload path/size/hash inventory; archive digest; extraction/install/generation/commissioning transcripts and receipts. Missing schema, stale output, absolute-source import, output escape, symlink/junction, or missing runtime file must fail. |
+| ORCH-ADOPT-01 | Exercise `loop:template:create`, frozen installation, `loop:commission`, the commissioning commit, literal generated `pnpm verify`, and `loop:template:prove` for two adopter definitions on Windows and Linux. Definitions differ in project/package IDs and target branch; one destination contains spaces. Generated goal/lock/profile/history belong to that adopter. | Generated file inventories, authority/base/commissioning identities, real bootstrap build/test/save/replay/Node/Worker evidence and Chromium screenshots/trace/diagnostics. Recommissioning, wrong authority, or omitted asset must reject. The source report links the generated bootstrap result as a compatibility sub-result and never upgrades its scope. |
+| ORCH-PLAN-01 | Through the production plan handler, admit one bounded proposal, preserve the protected floor, and reject an overbroad/protected/invalid proposal before a worker starts. Doctor and Status report the actual disposition and next action without mutating state. | Proposal JSON, policy/schema results, before/after state and refs, role invocation log and CLI diagnostic output. Valid IDs with malicious paths must not bypass semantic scope validation. |
+| ORCH-VERIFY-01 | Freeze a real modified candidate in a standalone clone and execute the commissioned candidate tier through the attested provider. Validate every receipt and raw artifact, canonical invariant first, all four owner partitions once, legacy subsumption only with its prerequisite, and fresh exact closure when required. | Tier plan/results, ownership discovery, partition inventories, command/timeout/provider records, source/fixture identity chain and final identity fence. Missing/forged/foreign/stale evidence and unclassified tests reject at the actual boundary; no integration follows. Hidden-validation authorization/custody is tested only with public synthetic records. |
+| ORCH-REVIEW-01 | The real review/integration handlers first refuse a rejected or stale review, then accept an independently produced structured review bound to the exact verified candidate and fast-forward only that candidate. Worker/reviewer processes and allowed inputs are separate. | Reviewer identity/input/diff record; structured decision; candidate verification; target ref transition; canonical completed state and cleanup intent. A worker-authored approval, review for another commit, or diverged target must not integrate. Deterministic role adapters establish mechanics; live quality is reserved for human acceptance. |
+| ORCH-STATE-01 | Persist actual lifecycle transitions through canonical generations; restart the controller and resume. Two synchronized same-generation writers produce exactly one successor. A contended lease has one owner. Repeat fixed logical inputs twice on both platforms and compare semantic state/decision traces. | Exact prior/current state bytes/hashes and private refs, generation/CAS/lease observations, restart traces and cross-platform comparison. Corrupt state, stale-generation writes, reused process identities and wrong candidates must not be adopted. Volatile telemetry is retained separately; normalization must never erase different decisions, scopes, artifact hashes, or success/failure. |
+| ORCH-RECOVERY-01 | Kill the owning process immediately before and after each durable publication boundary of workspace creation, candidate checkpoint preparation, target integration, cleanup, and approved retention. Resume via the production resume/recovery handlers. Exercise unamended v1, active v2, and reverse-amendment recovery compatibility. | Fault-point manifest, subprocess termination evidence, intent bytes, exact prior/new file/ref identities, resumed final state and equality to uninterrupted semantics. Every reachable publication boundary is enumerated by a checked-in registry; an unregistered added boundary fails discovery. Foreign files/refs remain untouched and diagnosed. |
+| ORCH-RECONCILE-01 | Perform a real external commit range in a disposable commissioned target and invoke `loop:reconcile`/status. Preserve the old state bytes, identify the complete continuous range, obtain fresh verification and independent structured reconciliation review, and adopt atomically. | Archived old state/hash; complete commit range; fresh exact candidate evidence; independent review and new state/ref identity. A missing/interrupted range, active intent, dirty target, substituted candidate or failed review blocks adoption without resetting history. |
+| ORCH-RETENTION-01 | Run retention preview, provide the fixture maintainer's explicit approval for the exact plan, and apply through the production retention operation. Complete normal workspace cleanup and resume an interrupted approved apply. | Retention plan/digest, separate approval record, dry-run inventory, before/after owned paths, intent/recovery records, undeleted sentinel files outside scope. No approval, stale plan, junction/symlink escape or foreign file permits deletion. Test actors may approve only their disposable fixture. |
+| ORCH-CONTAIN-01 | Run the real Docker executor with the pinned image and inspect the runtime's applied policy. Execute successful fixture build/tests plus outside-read, outside-write, network, privileged/socket, descendant-process, and resource/output-limit attacks. | Docker version/inspect and image digest; actual mounts/capabilities/resources/network; raw attack observations and owned receipts. No host checkout write, host secret/socket access, network success or surviving owned descendant is allowed. Missing runtime, wrong digest, extra mount/capability, unsupported policy, or diagnostic fallback is ineligible. |
+| ORCH-BOUNDS-01 | Exercise the production supervisor with output flood, inherited pipes, a stubborn descendant and deadline expiration; exercise read-only status/doctor over the resulting state. Show output capture stays within its actual cap, drain/termination facts are truthful, and the controller remains inspectable. | Raw byte/process observations, timeout and drain records, effective limits, diagnostic output, and no survivor evidence. Preserve current 64 MiB command-output cap, 5 s kill grace, 2 CPU/2 GiB/256 PID container limits, 65-minute owner tier bounds and 20-minute other focused tier bounds; individual test, exact-closure and measurement limits are unchanged. No speedup or general latency guarantee follows. |
+
+## Production release build
+
+The release is an allowlisted runtime/template distribution, not a copy of the working tree. Its payload contains the public CLI runtime (including the generator), schemas, configuration templates, bootstrap scaffold, necessary tools, package/workspace manifests, frozen lockfile, notices and user instructions. Preserve the currently supported TypeScript/tsx runtime layout; a new compiled SDK or npm publishing system is not required. Tests are packaged only where the installed controller, invariant/ownership discovery, generated verification or qualification workflow requires them; dependencies and runtime entrypoint discovery must prove the allowlist is complete.
+
+`build:production` writes `dist/milestone-loop-template/` plus a deterministic archive and release manifest under `dist/`. The manifest identifies the source commit/tree and each payload file's path, bytes, and hash. Source identity metadata is fixed for identical input commits; wall-clock build telemetry is outside the reproducible payload. No credentials, source `.git`, controller state, workspaces, evidence caches, source readiness marker, source commissioning state or source acceptance identity may leak into a generated adopter. The release's own provenance remains distinguishable from that adopter's identity.
+
+The archive is safely extracted into a fresh absent path, dependencies are installed from the frozen lock, and the extracted CLI is the only runtime used for the generation smoke. No source-checkout imports or writable shared node_modules are permitted. Repeated builds start with absent output roots and independent writable workspaces. The portable payload inventory must also agree between Windows and Linux; platform-specific dependency installations and execution telemetry are recorded outside that payload. Removing a declared runtime/schema/scaffold file from a committed mutation must break the external smoke. Build success requires the payload and its real consumer observations, not just `tsc --noEmit`, a tar operation, or unit success.
+
+## Qualification invocation and recursion boundary
+
+One literal no-argument source `pnpm verify` invocation owns a fresh cross-platform qualification identity and all required stages. A trusted external coordinator dispatches native Windows/Linux jobs for the full required observation matrix and validates their provenance and artifacts. Those jobs execute the source stage commands and real controller workflows; they do not recursively request source readiness. Generated adopters and bounded target fixtures execute their own distinct contracts. The source result cannot become completion-eligible until every required platform/provider observation has been independently validated.
+
+The tested controller needs a container control plane, so it runs on a maintainer-authorized disposable qualification host. Its candidate children remain in the existing attested OCI sandbox. Source candidate commands inside that sandbox cannot launch Docker or contact a qualification service. Where a source tier consumes remote workflow observations, the trusted outer coordinator must first produce a fresh, nonce-bound bundle and deliver it through a validated read-only task-input channel. The child command independently checks that bundle and owns a receipt for that verification; producer-owned workflow receipts remain separately retained. The outer verifier independently checks both. Candidate-authored files cannot act as coordinator approval or provider attestation. Missing coordinator, input-channel support or provenance remains NOT_READY, never host fallback. The reviewed qualification protocol specifies this additional implementation work.
+
+## Required negative scenarios
+
+The manifest names exactly sixteen scenario families, each mandatory on Windows and Linux. Each uses a real subprocess/command boundary and records its complete variant list: invalid proposal; protected-worker write; unclassified test; missing/forged child evidence; stale/rejected independent review; target divergence; stale/corrupt state; competing lease/generation; workspace-create crash; candidate-prepare crash; integration crash; cleanup escape/crash; unapproved/stale retention; external-reconciliation mismatch; amendment mixed/foreign/crashed generation; containment/supervision violation. Multi-boundary variants within a family all must pass; one representative cannot replace an omitted boundary.
+
+Required rate is 16/16 correct family dispositions and 100% of enumerated variants on each platform, with zero unexpected integrity violations. A deliberately rejected operation is not itself an integrity violation. Skips and infrastructure failures are not correct negative outcomes. This finite corpus is qualification evidence, not a 99% reliability estimate.
+
+## Human and live-agent acceptance
+
+After machine qualification, the maintainer uses the delivered distribution to create and commission a disposable adopter with a bounded real requirement and actual acceptance assertions. Use the committed live agent/model policy with separate Planner, Worker and Reviewer sessions. Exercise a successful change through verification, independent review and fast-forward integration; deliberately reject a stale/rejected candidate; terminate and resume one operation; inspect Status/Doctor and the evidence chain. Record actual transport and role identity, source release/adopter identities, commands, artifacts, review and maintainer disposition. Never substitute deterministic adapter output or scripted unit success for this live workflow.
+
+The maintainer verifies that instructions are sufficient, the integrated outcome meets the fixture's stated requirement, failed paths preserve data, and claim labels are understandable. Approval must be explicit and bound to the release digest. Missing credentials or service access leaves this gate pending. Human acceptance establishes the supported operator workflow, not arbitrary coding quality or downstream product completion.
+
+## Claim boundaries and historical requirements
+
+Generated bootstrap browser/Worker/persistence results remain mandatory compatibility observations within ORCH-ADOPT-01. They do not prove that the source has a game renderer or that the adopter's product is complete. The generated bootstrap profile remains completion-ineligible for autonomous readiness. The downstream readiness registry and frozen acceptance remain governed by that adopter's own contract.
+
+Old source AUTO/PLAY/VIS/PERF/REPLAY/SAVE/FAULT, metric, bot and seed IDs are not silently renamed. The approved migration mapping records each replacement, preservation, or source retirement. All original frozen documents, schemas, tests, threshold values and calibration history remain retrievable. Source-frame-time, game-seed and gameplay-bot requirements have no passing source outcome after this revision; their source applicability is explicitly retired. Neither this contract nor its qualification closes WP6e/WP6f or overwrites their evidence.
